@namespace Restaurant.UI.Shared.Components.Screens
@using System.Globalization
@using Restaurant.UI.Shared.Components.Upos
@using Restaurant.UI.Shared.Services
@implements IDisposable
@inject IDeviceStatus DeviceStatus

@* Handbook Part II-A · the terminal shell. "The terminal is one 1440×900 shell
   at --upos-radius-panel 26px, --upos-surface, clipped, with a fixed 76px top
   bar and a fixed 78px bottom nav. Everything between them is a destination."
   Order entry carries this spec because every destination inherits it, so the
   chrome is its own component and the destination arrives as ChildContent —
   the next seven terminal screens host into the same shell rather than
   re-drawing it.

   The shell fills its host. On Restaurant.Mobile that is the device viewport;
   in the back office's development preview that is a 1440×900 box. Nothing here
   hard-codes the artboard's dimensions, because the artboard's 1440×900 is one
   terminal, not the contract.

   The top bar carries three real readings and two blocked slots.

   Real:
   · the clock — the device's own time, ticking on a timer this component
     disposes.
   · the battery level and the connection state, both from IDeviceStatus (§12).
     They are here because the terminal now hides Android's status bar
     (Part II-A · Handheld, "The host's system bars"): that bar carried the
     clock, the signal and the battery, the clock was already here, and the
     other two had nowhere else to go. On the handheld, where the server name
     drops, the right of the bar is exactly the three things the status bar used
     to show.

   Blocked, in the Menu manager's treatment — quiet, on the inset, naming the
   gap, never an error:
   · the offline queue (GAP-10). §11's pill makes two claims and they became
     true at different times. "The terminal is offline" is observable now and
     the pill states it. "N writes are waiting to replay" is not, because no
     queue table exists, so the pill carries no count and the missing queue is
     named beside it. OFFLINE · 0 QUEUED would be the worst available answer:
     it is the sentence a healthy terminal with an empty queue also prints, so
     it cannot be read as a gap.
   · the server name (GAP-09) is a literal. There is no employee, role or shift
     record to read a name from, so the slot says so instead of inventing Maya.

   Nothing here renders a plausible-looking reading. Where IDeviceStatus answers
   null — the back office's preview, which is a browser with no device — the
   instrument cluster is replaced by one blocked chip saying so. §12 makes that
   the rule: render the reading, or render the absence of the reading, and never
   render a number nobody measured. *@

@{
    // Both readings are taken once per render and then used, rather than being
    // asked for at each of the four places the markup below needs them. They are
    // live properties over a platform API, so two reads inside one render can
    // disagree: the network dropping between the offline pill's test and the
    // instrument cluster's would draw a bar with a red pill and a connected
    // glyph on it. One snapshot cannot contradict itself, and the Changed event
    // brings the next one along immediately.
    var isOnline = DeviceStatus.IsOnline;
    var batteryLevel = DeviceStatus.BatteryLevel;
}

<div class="shell">

    @* ─── Top bar, 76px ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The one element in the product that takes --upos-blur-glass (§7):
       --upos-surface-veil under backdrop-filter, 1px --upos-border bottom,
       padding 0 28px. The artboard fills it flat with --upos-surface-inset; the
       veil is what makes the blur mean anything. *@
    <header class="shell__top">
        <div class="shell__identity">
            <span class="shell__mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="shell__venue">@Venue &middot; @TerminalId</span>

            @* §11's offline pill, in its permanent slot beside the venue name,
               and real for the half it can prove. It appears only when
               IDeviceStatus reports a known offline: an unknown reading is not
               a known offline, so the preview shows no pill and claims
               nothing. Online it is absent, which is §11's rule — the absence
               of the pill is the message.

               It reads OFFLINE and stops there. No count, no QUEUED. *@
            @if (isOnline == false)
            {
                <span class="u-pill-offline shell__offline">
                    <span class="shell__offline-mark" aria-hidden="true">@WifiOffIcon</span>
                    <span>OFFLINE</span>
                </span>
            }

            @* The queue behind §11's count — GAP-10, in position and inert.
               Part II-A · Handheld drops this chip below 800px and keeps the
               pill: of the two, the red pill states the terminal's condition
               and this one names a gap in the model, and on 393px of glass the
               condition wins. Both labels are in the DOM at every width and the
               container query picks one; neither is chosen in C#, because the
               shell's width is not a C# fact. *@
            <span class="shell__blocked shell__blocked--queue"
                  aria-disabled="true"
                  title="§11's pill reads OFFLINE · N QUEUED, where N counts writes waiting to replay. The connection is observed; the queue is not — no queue table exists, and with no idempotency key on CreateOrderDto a replayed write can double an order (GAP-10).">
                <span class="shell__blocked-wide">OFFLINE QUEUE &middot; GAP-10</span>
                <span class="shell__blocked-narrow">GAP-10</span>
            </span>
        </div>

        <div class="shell__status">
            @* Server name — GAP-09, in position and inert. It is the one thing
               the handheld top bar drops (Part II-A · Handheld): the person
               holding the device is the server. *@
            <span class="shell__blocked shell__blocked--server"
                  aria-disabled="true"
                  title="The server on shift comes from an employee record. The POC has no employee, role or shift model, so a name here would be a literal (GAP-09).">
                SERVER &middot; GAP-09
            </span>

            @* ─── Instrument cluster ───────────────────────────────────────
               Connectivity and battery, between the server and the clock, so
               identity reads left and instruments read right and the clock ends
               the bar where it always did.

               Each instrument draws only when it has a reading. A host that can
               read one and not the other draws the one it has; a host that can
               read neither draws the blocked chip below instead. That is why
               both readings are nullable rather than defaulting to zero and
               false: a host that cannot read a battery has not read a flat one,
               and a host that cannot see a network has not seen it fail. *@
            <span class="shell__instruments">
                @if (isOnline is bool online)
                {
                    <span class="shell__signal @(online ? null : "shell__signal--off")"
                          role="img"
                          aria-label="@(online ? "Network connected" : "No network")">
                        @(online ? WifiIcon : WifiOffIcon)
                    </span>
                }

                @if (batteryLevel is double level)
                {
                    var percent = (int)Math.Round(Math.Clamp(level, 0, 1) * 100);

                    @* The fill is the charge: the glyph's inner well runs from
                       x=4 to x=16, so 12 units span empty to full. Bound, not
                       stepped — this is a measurement, and rounding it into
                       four bars would be drawing less than was read. *@
                    <span class="shell__battery @(percent <= LowBatteryPercent ? "shell__battery--low" : null)"
                          role="img"
                          aria-label="@($"Battery {percent} percent")">
                        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                            <rect x="2" y="7" width="16" height="10" rx="2" />
                            <path d="M21 10v4" />
                            <rect x="4" y="9" width="@((12d * Math.Clamp(level, 0, 1)).ToString("0.##", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))" height="6" rx="1" fill="currentColor" stroke="none" />
                        </svg>
                        <span class="shell__battery-level">@percent%</span>
                    </span>
                }

                @* Neither reading available. One chip for both, in the blocked
                   treatment, in the cluster's own place — so the bar says which
                   instruments are missing and why, rather than falling silent
                   and reading like a terminal that is simply fine. *@
                @if (isOnline is null && batteryLevel is null)
                {
                    <span class="shell__blocked"
                          aria-disabled="true"
                          title="Battery level and connection state come from the device, through IDeviceStatus (§12). This host has no device to read — it is a browser standing the terminal's components up at the artboard's size — so it reports nothing rather than drawing a plausible battery.">
                        <span class="shell__blocked-wide">BATTERY AND SIGNAL &middot; NO DEVICE READING</span>
                        <span class="shell__blocked-narrow">NO READING</span>
                    </span>
                }
            </span>

            <span class="shell__clock">@_now.ToString("HH:mm")</span>
        </div>
    </header>

    @* ─── Destination ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Everything between the two bars. It takes the remaining height and owns
       its own scrolling; nothing in the shell scrolls. *@
    <main class="shell__body">
        @ChildContent
    </main>

    @* ─── Bottom nav, 78px ────────────────────────────────────────────────
       ORDER · TABLES · PAYMENTS · CHANNELS · MORE. Order entry and MORE are
       the two destinations built in this pass, so the other three render inert
       rather than answering a tap with nothing (§11 makes this nav the only
       full-screen transition in the flow, which is exactly why an item that
       goes nowhere cannot look live). *@
    <BottomNav Items="Destinations"
               Active="@ActiveDestination"
               OnNavigate="OnNavigate"
               Label="Terminal destinations" />
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The venue's name, left of the terminal id in the top bar.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Venue { get; set; } = "Riverside Grill";

    /// <summary>This terminal's id — the artboard's "Counter 2".</summary>
    [Parameter] public string TerminalId { get; set; } = "Counter 2";

    /// <summary>The destination between the bars.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The active destination's key. Exactly one is active.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string ActiveDestination { get; set; } = "order";

    /// <summary>Raised when a built destination is tapped. ORDER and MORE are built
    /// in this pass, so this fires with "order" or "more" and nothing else. The host
    /// owns which destination is on screen; the shell only reports the tap.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string> OnNavigate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The charge at or below which the battery inks --upos-status-late-text. §4:
    /// red marks a problem that exists, and a terminal about to die mid-service is
    /// one. Part II-A records the 20 as a policy literal rather than a token — it is
    /// a threshold a condition tests, not a value a declaration takes, so §12's
    /// add-the-token rule does not reach it, exactly as it does not reach the
    /// handheld step's 800.
    /// </summary>
    private const int LowBatteryPercent = 20;

    private DateTime _now = DateTime.Now;
    private Timer? _clock;

    /// <summary>
    /// The five destinations. Three carry Enabled false: the floor plan, payment and
    /// channels are specced in Part II-A and not built in this pass. MORE is built —
    /// it is the terminal's overflow and it opens Printer setup, which is where a
    /// Bluetooth pairing has to be made because a bond is a fact about this radio and
    /// this piece of glass (Part II-A · Printer setup).
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<NavItem> Destinations => new[]
    {
        new NavItem("order", "ORDER", OrderIcon) { Enabled = Reachable("order") },
        new NavItem("tables", "TABLES", TablesIcon) { Enabled = false },
        new NavItem("payments", "PAYMENTS", PaymentsIcon) { Enabled = false },
        new NavItem("channels", "CHANNELS", ChannelsIcon) { Enabled = false },
        new NavItem("more", "MORE", MoreIcon) { Enabled = Reachable("more") }
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a built destination can be reached from this shell.
    ///
    /// Being built is not the same as being reachable, and the difference is the
    /// host. Restaurant.Mobile wires OnNavigate and both built destinations answer a
    /// tap; the back office's two previews stand one destination up in a fixed frame
    /// and wire nothing, so a tap there has nowhere to go and the item renders inert
    /// rather than doing nothing quietly — the same rule the three unbuilt
    /// destinations follow.
    ///
    /// The active destination is exempt because you are already on it: it has to keep
    /// the fill that says so, and tapping where you already are is not a tap that goes
    /// nowhere.
    /// </summary>
    private bool Reachable(string key) =>
        OnNavigate.HasDelegate || string.Equals(key, ActiveDestination, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

    /// <summary>
    /// The clock is wall time on the device, so it has to move. Half a minute is
    /// close enough for a bar that reads HH:mm and cheap enough not to matter; the
    /// timer starts after the first render, so it never fires into a component that
    /// has not rendered, and it is disposed with the component.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnAfterRender(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender)
        {
            return;
        }

        _clock = new Timer(_ =>
        {
            _now = DateTime.Now;
            _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        }, null, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30), TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The battery and the connection are pushed, not polled: the host raises
    /// Changed off the platform's own callbacks, so the bar re-renders when a
    /// reading actually moves and at no other time. A host with nothing to report
    /// never raises it, which is why the preview costs nothing.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnInitialized() => DeviceStatus.Changed += OnDeviceStatusChanged;

    private void OnDeviceStatusChanged(object? sender, EventArgs e) => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    public void Dispose()
    {
        DeviceStatus.Changed -= OnDeviceStatusChanged;
        _clock?.Dispose();
    }

    // Top-bar instruments: 15px inline stroke SVG, §9's dense band. wifi and
    // wifi-off are both on §9's expected POS glyph list; the paths are Lucide's, on
    // the same 24px grid and the same stroke model, pasted inline as §9 requires.
    private RenderFragment WifiIcon => @<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12.55a11 11 0 0 1 14.08 0" /><path d="M1.42 9a16 16 0 0 1 21.16 0" /><path d="M8.53 16.11a6 6 0 0 1 6.95 0" /><path d="M12 20h.01" /></svg>;

    private RenderFragment WifiOffIcon => @<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 1l22 22" /><path d="M16.72 11.06A10.94 10.94 0 0 1 19 12.55" /><path d="M5 12.55a10.94 10.94 0 0 1 5.17-2.39" /><path d="M10.71 5.05A16 16 0 0 1 22.58 9" /><path d="M1.42 9a15.91 15.91 0 0 1 4.7-2.88" /><path d="M8.53 16.11a6 6 0 0 1 6.95 0" /><path d="M12 20h.01" /></svg>;

    // Icons: 17px inline stroke SVG at stroke-width 2, currentColor (§9).
    private RenderFragment OrderIcon => @<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 4h16v5H4zM4 13h16v7H4z" /><path d="M8 17h4" /></svg>;

    private RenderFragment TablesIcon => @<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="6" width="18" height="9" rx="2" /><path d="M7 15v4M17 15v4" /></svg>;

    private RenderFragment PaymentsIcon => @<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="2" y="5" width="20" height="14" rx="2" /><path d="M2 10h20" /></svg>;

    private RenderFragment ChannelsIcon => @<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3" /><path d="M12 2v4M12 18v4M2 12h4M18 12h4" /></svg>;

    private RenderFragment MoreIcon => @<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="5" cy="12" r="1.4" /><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="1.4" /><circle cx="19" cy="12" r="1.4" /></svg>;
}
